There are a lot of issues with the data of diamonds, first off just by looking
at the residuals by fitted values for price ~ carat, 
that should look like a fat sausage around 0 and no apparent pattern. 
But you can definitely see a clear downward slope where
there's definitely a ton of outliers that mean most of the residuals are 
negative, and they are certainly not clustered around 0. You can see this 
wonky relationship with the carat ~ price relationship plot, 
where it's more like the right side of a parabola relationship rather than 
a linear relationship so we're breaking our assumption of linearity. 
Then there are the outliers of only a few high carat diamonds that
cost a lot of money but fall outside of the parabolic-like relationship. Then 
you can see the relationship that cut has on price, where the better the cut
(fair being worst and ideal being best) causes the price relationship
to be much steeper, i.e. price rises much faster as you increase carat for
an ideal diamond than fair diamonds. You can see the extreme right skew 
as well for the histogram looking at the count for each priced diamond. Also,
there are some dimensions that are 0, which doesn't make any sense for a 
diamond to have any of their dimensions be 0. There also seems to be
duplicates or overcounting in the data, which can lead to obscuring
true relationships between variables when there are so many overlapping
observations. 



