## make plots and describe


ggpairs(
      Boston,
      columns = c("medv", "lstat", "rm", "dis", "zn"),
      aes(color = factor(chas)), 
      lower = list(continuous = "smooth"), 
      diag = list(continuous = "density") 
    )

ggplot(data=Boston, mapping=aes(medv, lstat)) +
  geom_point()

ggplot(data=Boston, mapping=aes(dis, nox)) +
  geom_point()

ggplot(data=Boston, mapping=aes(lstat, nox)) +
  geom_point()




```

Some relationships I see are a somewhat strong negative linear relationship
between medv and lstat, meaning as you increase the median value of 
owner-occupied homes in $1000s, the lower status of the population (percent) 
goes down. Which makes sense as the more expensive the property the more 
wealthy people will be living there, or rather less "lower status" population.
Also a somewhat linear negative relationship between dis and nox, so
as the weighted mean of distances to five Boston employment centres increases,
or the farther you get from these centres, the lower the nitrogen oxides
concetration (ppm) becomes since you're farther from these high oxide emitting
centres you are. Finally another relationship I see is a weak positive
relationship between lstat and nox, so the larger proportion of lower status
of the population there is, the higher the nitrogen oxides concentration 
there is (though not the strongest linear relationship).


