Next fit linear models using the `lm()` function:

(a) For each predictor fit a simple linear regression model to predict the response. Describe your results. In which of the models is there a statistically significant association between the predictor and the response?

```{r}


response <- "medv"


predictors <- setdiff(names(Boston), response)


for (pred in predictors) {
 
  formula <- as.formula(paste(response, "~", pred))
  

  model <- lm(formula, data = Boston)
  
 
  cat("\n--- Predictor:", pred, "---\n")

 
  print(summary(model))
}


```
It appears that in all of the models predicting median value of owner-occupied
homes in $1000s there is a statistically significant association 
between predictors and medv. But the R squared value is pretty low for 
all of them, meaning there is a lot of noise and likely not a very 
strong linear relationship between the predictor and response. Ie between 
crim and medv, the R squared value is .15, so only 15% of the variability
in medv is explained by crim. 

(b) Fit a multiple regression model to predict the response using all of the predictors. Describe your results (including diagnostic plots). For which predictors can we reject the null hypothesis $H_0: \beta_j = 0$?

```{r}
mlr_model <- lm(medv ~ crim + zn + indus + chas + nox + rm + age + dis + rad + tax + ptratio + black + lstat, data = Boston)

summary(mlr_model)

residuals <- residuals(mlr_model)
fitted_values <- fitted(mlr_model)
    
plot(fitted_values, residuals)

qqnorm(residuals)

```
We can reject the null hypothesis for all of the predictors except age and indus.
The QQ plot looks somewhat reasonable, showing somewhat of a downward
bow in the middle but for the most part straight and diagonal, which 
means it does not break any of our assumptions. The fitted values by residual
plot is definitely a mess though and does not have an expected value of 0 
as there are many points above 0 that aren't balanced out. The
majority of the points are around 0 but it does seem to break our assumption
of homoscedasticity as there is a slight bow in our plot plus outliers. 


(c) How do your results from (a) compare to your results from (b)? Create a plot displaying the univariate regression coefficients from (a) on the $x$-axis and the multiple regression coefficients from (b) on the $y$-axis. That is, each predictor is displayed as a single point on the plot. Its coefficient in a simple linear regression model is shown as its $x$ coordinate and its coefficient in a multiple linear regression model is shown as its $y$ coordinate. Describe what you see.

```{r}


simple_coefs <- numeric(length(predictors))

for (i in seq_along(predictors)) {
  pred <- predictors[i]
  model <- lm(as.formula(paste(response, "~", pred)), data = Boston)
  simple_coefs[i] <- coef(model)[2]  
}

multi_model <- lm(medv ~ ., data = Boston)
multi_coefs <- coef(multi_model)[predictors] 

coef_df <- data.frame(
  Predictor = predictors,
  Simple = simple_coefs,
  Multiple = as.numeric(multi_coefs)
)

ggplot(coef_df, aes(x = Simple, y = Multiple, label = Predictor)) +
  geom_point(color = "blue", size = 3) +

  geom_text(vjust = -0.5, size = 3) +

  labs(
    x = "Simple Regression Coefficient",
    y = "Multiple Regression Coefficient",
    title = "Comparison of Simple vs. Multiple Regression Coefficients"
  )
```
It appears that nox has a strong negative slope alone (very far left), but its
slope becomes less negative in the multiple model (much higher on the y-axis).
rm (average rooms) stays very positive in both, which makes sense as it’s 
consistently a strong predictor. Most other variables are clustered near the 
center (doesn't look like the center due to nox skewing the plot), 
which means their unique effect shrinks once you control for the others. This 
plot shows signs of multicollinearity, where predictors that seem powerful
in explaining the response on their own can shrink when adjusted for 
other responses since they are overlapping in what they explain. 

 
